/*
 * TBuilder.h.ct - AST -> TIR (S1b-S1, docs/design/tir-lir-contract.md §1-§3).
 *
 * The builder is the one place that knows how an AST node becomes a typed TIR
 * row. It reads the node pool, the symbol tables and the type pass, and it
 * writes only into TModule. Nothing here emits C and nothing may touch the
 * node pool: the shadow is read-only with respect to the AST -> C path, which
 * is why `CT_TIR=1` provably does not change one output byte.
 *
 * Scope handling: the parser pops locals and parameters when a function ends,
 * so by the time TIR runs the symbol table only has file-scope rows. The
 * builder therefore re-walks declarations in source order and keeps its own
 * scope stack, exactly the shape the emitter's shadow bindings use. A local
 * `T` reference resolves to a `ct_tvar` id (RT_VAR); a global resolves to its
 * TDecl.
 *
 * Ownership: the parser appends one `delete v;` for every `own` (and one for
 * `borrow`) at the block exit, and copies the declaration's line onto it
 * (StmtDefer.h.ct autodrop_append). S1 uses that line match to attribute a
 * delete to the declaration and to register the matching drop / handback
 * cleanup entry; a user-written `delete` keeps its own line and stays an
 * ordinary statement. This is recorded as a fact, not guessed at emit time.
 *
 * Fault injection: `CT_TIR_FAULT=<key>` is the test seam for the verifier
 * negatives (contract §5.2). It appends one deliberately malformed TIR row
 * before verification and exists only under the shadow switch, so it can
 * never affect a normal compile.
 *
 * License: same as the rest of the compiler (see LICENSE / LICENSE.EXCEPTION).
 */
